From the late eighteenth century, the Company also used policies short of direct conquest.

**Subsidiary Alliance** (devised by **Lord Wellesley**, Governor-General 1798–1805). An Indian ruler who accepted it:

- could not have independent armed forces;
- would be protected by the Company, but had to **pay for the subsidiary forces** the Company was supposed to maintain for this protection;
- had to accept a British **Resident** at his court;
- could not make agreements with other rulers without Company permission.

If a ruler failed to pay, part of his territory was taken away as a penalty. **Awadh** was forced to give over half its territory in 1801; **Hyderabad** also ceded territories.

**Paramountcy.** Under **Lord Hastings** (Governor-General 1813–1823), the Company claimed that its authority was paramount, or supreme — so it could annex or threaten to annex any kingdom to protect its interests. Rulers who resisted faced war. **Rani Channamma** of **Kittur** (in Karnataka) led an armed resistance against the British in 1824 when they tried to annex her state; she was arrested and died in prison in 1829.

**Doctrine of Lapse** (under **Lord Dalhousie**, Governor-General 1848–1856): if an Indian ruler died without a male heir, his kingdom would 'lapse' — become part of Company territory. **Satara (1848), Sambalpur (1850), Udaipur (1852), Nagpur (1853) and Jhansi (1854)** were annexed this way. Finally, in **1856**, the Company also took over **Awadh**, claiming it was obliged to free the people from 'misgovernment'. These annexations later fed the anger of 1857.

**Punjab.** **Maharaja Ranjit Singh** had built a powerful Sikh kingdom and kept the Company at bay. After his death in **1839**, two **Anglo-Sikh Wars** (1845–46 and 1848–49) were fought, and **Punjab was annexed in 1849**. **Sindh** had been taken in 1843.

**Coins** are among the most useful sources. India's earliest coins, from about the **sixth century BCE**, are **punch-marked coins** — pieces of silver or copper with symbols punched on them. Later, rulers such as the **Indo-Greeks**, **Kushanas** and **Guptas** issued coins with **names and portraits** of kings; the Guptas issued magnificent **gold coins**.

From a coin, a historian can learn:

- the **ruler's name and titles**, and sometimes a date;
- the **extent of a kingdom** or its trade (where coins are found);
- the **economy** — gold, silver or copper, and the purity of metal (falling purity may show economic trouble);
- **beliefs** — images of gods, goddesses or symbols;
- **contacts** — Roman gold coins found in south India show trade with Rome about 2,000 years ago.

**Deciphering scripts.** In the nineteenth century, nobody could read the Brahmi script on Ashoka's pillars. In **1837–38**, **James Prinsep**, an official of the East India Company's mint in Calcutta, deciphered Brahmi, using inscriptions and **bilingual coins** of Indo-Greek kings (with Greek on one side and an Indian script on the other). Suddenly, Ashoka's voice could be heard again. Kharosthi was deciphered around the same time. Yet the **Harappan script** — on thousands of seals — remains undeciphered: there is no bilingual text, and inscriptions are very short.

**Vardhamana**, later called **Mahavira** ('great hero'), was born at **Kundagrama near Vaishali**, traditionally dated 599 BCE (many historians prefer a somewhat later date). He was a prince of the Jnatrika clan. At about 30 he left home, and after some twelve years of hard austerity he attained **kevala jnana**, complete knowledge, and became a **Jina** ('conqueror' of the self). His followers are called **Jainas**.

Jains regard Mahavira as the **24th tirthankara** ('ford-maker' who helps people cross the river of rebirth). The 23rd, **Parshvanatha**, is believed to have lived earlier.

Mahavira taught that every living being, even plants, insects and water, has a soul (*jiva*), and that karma clings to the soul like dust. Liberation comes by burning away karma through right living. The **three jewels** (*triratna*): **right faith, right knowledge, right conduct**. The **five vows**:

1. **Ahimsa** — non-violence to all living beings;
2. **Satya** — truthfulness;
3. **Asteya** — not stealing;
4. **Aparigraha** — non-possession;
5. **Brahmacharya** — chastity (often said to be added by Mahavira).

Jains also developed the idea of **anekantavada** — that truth has many sides, and no single viewpoint captures all of it (the famous parable of the blind men and the elephant is often used to explain it).

Because farming harms small creatures, many Jains became **traders**, which helped Jainism spread among merchant communities. Mahavira preached in **Prakrit** (Ardhamagadhi), the language of ordinary people, not Sanskrit. He passed away at **Pavapuri** near Rajgir, around the age of 72.

Later, Jainism split into two main traditions: **Digambara** ('sky-clad', monks renounce clothing) and **Shvetambara** ('white-clad'). Jain teachings were compiled at a council at **Valabhi** in Gujarat around the 5th–6th century CE. Jainism spread to Gujarat, Rajasthan and Karnataka, where the giant statue of **Gommateshwara** at Shravanabelagola (10th century CE) still stands.

Around **321 BCE**, a young man named **Chandragupta Maurya** overthrew the last Nanda king of Magadha. Tradition says he was guided by a sharp Brahmin teacher known as **Chanakya** or **Kautilya**, once insulted at the Nanda court. Stories of their partnership grew into legend (and, much later, into Vishakhadatta's play *Mudrarakshasa*), so historians handle the details carefully. But the outcome is certain: within a generation, the Mauryas ruled the largest empire India had yet seen, stretching from Afghanistan to Bengal and deep into the Deccan.

How do we know about the Mauryas? This is one of the best-documented periods of ancient India:

- **Ashoka's inscriptions** — the emperor's own words, carved on rocks and pillars;
- **Kautilya's Arthashastra** — a treatise on statecraft (scholars debate how much of the surviving text dates to Mauryan times; parts may be later);
- **Megasthenes' Indica** — the Greek ambassador's account, surviving only as quotations in later writers such as Strabo and Arrian;
- **Buddhist and Jain texts** and Sri Lankan chronicles (*Dipavamsa, Mahavamsa*);
- **archaeology**: Pataliputra's wooden palisade, polished pillars, coins.

What was Ashoka's **dhamma** (Sanskrit *dharma*)? It was not a demand that everyone become Buddhist, although Ashoka personally became a Buddhist lay follower. It was a **code of moral conduct** for everyone in the empire:

- respect for parents, elders and teachers;
- kindness to slaves and servants;
- generosity to Brahmins and shramanas (monks of all sects);
- **tolerance** of other sects: Ashoka urged people not to praise their own sect while condemning others;
- **ahimsa**: limiting the killing of animals; the royal kitchen cut down on meat;
- avoiding useless rituals.

To spread it, Ashoka:

- appointed **dhamma-mahamattas**, special officers to promote dhamma and help people;
- went on **dhamma tours** (*dhamma-yatras*) instead of hunting trips;
- had wells dug, rest houses built, **banyan trees planted along roads**, and medical treatment arranged for people and animals;
- sent missions abroad; tradition says his son **Mahinda** and daughter **Sanghamitra** carried Buddhism to **Sri Lanka**. His edicts mention Greek kings of West Asia, Egypt and beyond.

The **Third Buddhist Council** at Pataliputra, traditionally held under Ashoka with the monk Moggaliputta Tissa presiding, is described in Sri Lankan chronicles.

Historians debate Ashoka's motives: sincere conversion, a clever way to unite a diverse empire, or both. Most agree that his inscriptions show a ruler thinking seriously about the welfare of his subjects, centuries ahead of his time.

From 1885 to about 1905 the Congress was led by men usually called the **Moderates**: **Dadabhai Naoroji**, **Pherozeshah Mehta**, **Surendranath Banerjea**, **Gopal Krishna Gokhale**, **Badruddin Tyabji**, **M. G. Ranade** and others.

**What they demanded:**
- more Indians in the legislative councils, with real powers;
- civil service examinations to be held in India as well as London;
- reduction of military expenditure and land revenue;
- protection of Indian industries; freedom of speech and press.

**How they worked:** resolutions, petitions, speeches and newspapers — the method critics nicknamed *prayer, petition and protest*. They believed British justice could be appealed to.

**Their great contribution** was an **economic critique of colonialism**. **Dadabhai Naoroji**'s *Poverty and Un-British Rule in India* (1901) set out the **drain theory**: Indian wealth flowed to Britain through home charges, salaries, pensions and profits. **R. C. Dutt** wrote *The Economic History of India*. This argument showed that poverty in India was linked to colonial policy, not fate.

Dadabhai Naoroji was also the first Indian elected to the **British House of Commons** (1892, for Finsbury Central).
